Merging three separate support teams in a city administration into one desk, without adding headcount.

Three departments each ran their own support line. Residents were passed from one number to the next, and the same question was often answered three times in a week. Nobody could say how long a typical request took from start to finish.

We spent the first weeks listening to the agents and to the people who called. We recorded calls, sorted requests by topic and measured how many were solved at the first contact. The answer was about one in three, which gave us a clear goal for the design.

The new desk has one number, one queue and one set of answer scripts. Agents are grouped by skill, not by department, and a short shared knowledge base answers the most common questions. We trained the agents in two groups, so that the desk could keep working during the change.

The first month brought fewer tickets, not more, and the average wait fell by about 40 percent. The city now reviews the desk every quarter, and the three former teams share one manager.
